#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# executable-bit - every tracked file whose first line is a shebang is committed 100755.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXISTS, AND IT IS NOT A TIDINESS RULE.
#
# tests/bin/gate-a-wave1.sh and tests/bin/gate-a-wave3.sh guard every invariant they run with
# `if [ -x "$INV" ]`. That test asks the FILESYSTEM. On the Windows checkout this project is
# developed in, git is configured not to track the executable bit at all, so the filesystem
# answers TRUE for a file committed 100644 and the guard runs the invariant. On the runner,
# which checks the file out of the index at the mode the index records, the same test answers
# FALSE, the guard takes its ELSE branch, and the gate reports the invariant as missing.
#
# The result is the worst shape a local check can have: it is QUIETER THAN THE GATE. The runner
# reads "N checks - 0 failures" here and "N checks - M failures" there, on the same commit, and
# nothing on this side can tell you which one is real.
#
# MEASURED IN THE SIBLING REPOSITORY, NOT PREDICTED. Pipeline 950124 of agora_theme, ref 1.x,
# commit b1744fa: the agora-invariants job failed while the other nine passed, because three
# scripts had been committed 100644. The local gate was green for a full push cycle. This
# repository carries the SAME guard in both runners - 1 use in wave 1 and 14 in wave 3 - and
# had no such check until this file existed.
#
# IT WAS NOT VACUOUS ON ITS FIRST RUN HERE, which is the part worth keeping. On the commit that
# introduced it this tree held 448 tracked files, 28 of them starting with a shebang, and TWO of
# those 28 were committed 100644: tests/bin/content-timestamps.py and
# tests/bin/generate-demo-media.py. Neither was breaking the gate today, because G15 reaches
# generate-demo-media.py through `[ -r ]` and `python3 <path>` rather than through `[ -x ]` and
# direct execution - so the defect was real, latent, and one refactor away from being a red
# runner nobody could reproduce locally. Both were corrected with `git update-index --chmod=+x`
# in the same commit as this file. A check whose first run finds something is a check that was
# worth writing; this one's first finding is recorded here so nobody later assumes it has never
# had one.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE MODE IS READ FROM THE GIT INDEX. NEVER FROM THE FILESYSTEM.
#
# That single sentence is the whole content of this check on this host. `git ls-files -s` prints
# the mode the index records, which is the mode the runner will check out and the mode a
# reviewer downloading the package will get. `ls -l`, `test -x` and `stat` all answer a
# different question here, and they answer it wrongly. If this file is ever "simplified" to use
# one of them it will pass on every machine and stop meaning anything.
#
# THE FIRST LINE IS READ FROM THE INDEX TOO, with `git grep --cached`. Staged-but-uncommitted
# content is what the next commit will contain, so a check that read the working tree would be
# answering about a state nobody is about to push.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SELECTOR IS THE SHEBANG, NOT A DIRECTORY AND NOT AN EXTENSION.
#
# A rule written as "everything under tests/bin" covers today's scripts and silently fails to
# cover the first one somebody puts anywhere else. A rule written as "*.sh" fails on this
# project's own naming, where most of the scripts have no extension at all and six are python
# helpers that shell invariants call. What actually makes a file need the bit is that the kernel
# is expected to run it, and the mark of that is the shebang on line one. So that is the
# selector, and the sweep is the WHOLE tracked tree.
#
# Widening it from tests/bin to the repository changes no number today: all 28 shebang files are
# in tests/bin. That is stated rather than left implied, because a rule whose scope was widened
# without changing its result is exactly the kind of change somebody quietly narrows again
# later, having measured only today.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO DENOMINATORS, AND THEY ARE NOT ONE QUESTION ASKED TWICE.
#
#   examined  comes from `git ls-files`, so it stays positive even if the shebang selector broke.
#   scripts   comes from the selector, so it can only be positive if first lines were read.
#
# "448 files examined, 0 findings" from a selector that matched nothing is the exact shape of
# green this refuses (I-028). Both counts are printed on every run and both are asserted by the
# gate runner beside the exit code.
#
# ONE IMPLEMENTATION, TWO CALLERS. tests/bin/gate-a-wave1.sh runs this as G10, and
# tests/bin/preflight runs it as the first half of its agora-invariants coverage. Neither
# carries its own copy of the rule. Two copies of one rule disagree eventually, and the
# disagreement is invisible because both print a plausible number.
#
# IT IS IN WAVE 1 AND NOT WAVE 3 ON PURPOSE. It is pure git: no network, no container, no
# database, and it returns in well under a second. Wave 3 takes about 35 minutes, and a guard
# against a defect this cheap to detect is a guard that gets skipped if it is only affordable
# half an hour at a time.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS RULE ALSO EXISTS IN agora_theme, AND THAT IS A D-028 QUESTION WITH AN OWNER.
#
# tests/bin/executable-bit was WRITTEN in agora_theme and ported here, which is the opposite of
# the direction D-028's manifest models - there, agora_transparency is always the source and the
# theme holds the copy. The theme's tests/bin/shared-invariants currently lists this script in
# its LOCAL_ONLY set, justified in its own prose with the sentence "the site template has no
# copy of it". That sentence became FALSE the moment this file landed.
#
# Nothing goes red on either side: LOCAL_ONLY membership is what the theme's invariant checks,
# and the justification beside it is a comment. That is precisely the hazard - a stale claim
# that no mechanism can contradict. Recorded here, in the file the next reader will be standing
# in, and reported for routing rather than fixed by reaching into the other repository, because
# D-028 forbids resolving a shared-file question from one side alone.
#
# HOUSE RULES (T-321(c)): `wc -l` never `grep -c` for a compared value; a grep exit status of 2
# or more is an ERROR, never "no match".
#
# Usage:  bash tests/bin/executable-bit
# Exit:   0 clean - 1 findings, or the run could not be made meaningful.

set -u

C_OK=""; C_BAD=""; C_DIM=""; C_OFF=""
if [ -t 1 ]; then C_OK=$'\033[32m'; C_BAD=$'\033[31m'; C_DIM=$'\033[2m'; C_OFF=$'\033[0m'; fi

REPO_ROOT=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")/../.." && pwd)
cd "$REPO_ROOT" || { printf 'FATAL: cannot reach the repository root\n' >&2; exit 1; }

FINDINGS=0

finding() { # <file> <mode> <reason>
  FINDINGS=$((FINDINGS + 1))
  printf '  %s%s:%s%s  %s\n' "$C_BAD" "$1" "$2" "$C_OFF" "$3"
}

note() { printf '  %s%s%s\n' "$C_DIM" "$1" "$C_OFF"; }

fatal() { # <reason>
  printf '\n%sFATAL:%s %s\n' "$C_BAD" "$C_OFF" "$1"
  printf 'This run has NO result, which is not a clean result (I-029).\n'
  exit 1
}

printf '=========================================================================================================\n'
printf 'executable-bit - a tracked file that starts with a shebang is committed 100755\n'
printf 'repo: %s\n' "$REPO_ROOT"
printf 'date: %s\n' "$(date -u '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ')"
printf '=========================================================================================================\n'

command -v git >/dev/null 2>&1 || fatal 'git not found - the mode this check reads exists only in the index'
git rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree >/dev/null 2>&1 || fatal 'not inside a git work tree'

WORKDIR=$(mktemp -d -t executable-bit.XXXXXX) || fatal 'mktemp -d failed'
trap 'rm -rf "$WORKDIR"' EXIT INT TERM

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the tracked file set ---
git ls-files > "$WORKDIR/tracked" 2>/dev/null || fatal 'git ls-files failed'
EXAMINED=$(wc -l < "$WORKDIR/tracked" | tr -d ' \r')
[ "${EXAMINED:-0}" -gt 0 ] || fatal 'the index lists 0 tracked files - the result would prove nothing (I-007)'

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- the shebang selector ---
# `--cached` is what makes this read the index. `-I` skips binary blobs, so a font, a PDF or an
# image whose first bytes happen to be those two characters is never offered to the mode test -
# and this package ships demo media, so that guard is load-bearing here rather than theoretical.
# The `:1:` filter is what makes it FIRST-line: git grep reports every matching line, and a
# shebang on line 40 is a comment, not an interpreter directive.
git grep --cached -I -n -E '^#!' -- . > "$WORKDIR/hits" 2>/dev/null
GREP_RC=$?
if [ "$GREP_RC" -ge 2 ]; then
  fatal "git grep exited $GREP_RC while selecting shebang files - a tree that could not be searched is NOT a tree that is correct (I-027)"
fi

sed -n 's/^\(.*\):1:#!.*$/\1/p' < "$WORKDIR/hits" | LC_ALL=C sort -u > "$WORKDIR/scripts"
SCRIPTS=$(wc -l < "$WORKDIR/scripts" | tr -d ' \r')

printf '\n'
note 'scope: every tracked file, first line and mode both read from the git index, never from the filesystem'

# I-028 canary. If the selector breaks - a git that spells --cached differently, a sed that stops
# matching - this file reports 0 findings over 0 scripts and looks exactly like a clean tree.
if [ "${SCRIPTS:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
  fatal 'the shebang selector matched 0 files. This repository ships the shell invariants and python helpers under tests/bin/ that both gate runners execute, so zero means the selector has broken, not that the tree is clean (I-028).'
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------- the mode assertion ---
while IFS= read -r f; do
  [ -n "$f" ] || continue
  MODE=$(git ls-files -s -- "$f" 2>/dev/null | cut -d' ' -f1)
  if [ -z "$MODE" ]; then
    finding "$f" '<no mode>' 'the index has no mode for this path although it was listed as tracked - the two git reads disagree and this run cannot be trusted'
    continue
  fi
  [ "$MODE" = '100755' ] && continue
  finding "$f" "$MODE" "starts with a shebang but is committed $MODE. On this host the executable-bit test answers TRUE for it and every local check passes; on the runner it answers FALSE. Fix with: git update-index --chmod=+x $f   (git add --chmod=+x for a file not yet tracked)."
done < "$WORKDIR/scripts"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- summary ---
printf '\n'
printf 'examined: %s tracked file(s)\n' "$EXAMINED"
printf 'scripts:  %s with a shebang on line 1\n' "$SCRIPTS"
printf 'findings: %s\n' "$FINDINGS"
printf '=========================================================================================================\n'

if [ "$FINDINGS" -eq 0 ]; then
  printf '%s%s%s\n' "$C_OK" 'executable-bit: clean' "$C_OFF"
  exit 0
fi
printf '%s%s%s%s%s\n' "$C_BAD" 'executable-bit: ' "$FINDINGS" ' finding(s)' "$C_OFF"
exit 1
